Template: kismet-common/install-setuid
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Should Kismet be installed with suid-root helpers?
 Kismet uses multiple helper programs to capture packets.  Some helpers,
 such as the Linux Wi-Fi and Bluetooth capture tools, need elevated
 privileges to reconfigure interfaces and capture packets.
 .
 Kismet can be installed so that members of the "kismet" system group can
 run these helpers with the privileges they need, allowing the Kismet
 server itself to run as a normal user.
 .
 This is more secure than running Kismet as root, which increases the risk
 if there is a security error in Kismet.
 .
 This setting is shared by all Kismet capture packages.  If you change it
 with dpkg-reconfigure, apply it to every installed capture helper with:
 .
   dpkg-reconfigure -f noninteractive $(dpkg -l 'kismet-capture-*' | awk '/^ii/ {print $2}')
 .
 For more detailed information, please see the Kismet README at:
 https://www.kismetwireless.net/docs/readme/intro/kismet/

Template: kismet-common/device-access
Type: select
Choices: udev, suid
Default: udev
Description: How should Kismet USB and serial capture helpers access devices?
 Many Kismet capture helpers only need access to a USB or serial device,
 such as a Bluetooth, Zigbee, or SDR sniffer.
 .
 udev: Install udev rules which give members of the "kismet" system group
 access to supported devices.  The capture helpers run without any extra
 privileges.  This is the recommended option.
 .
 suid: Install the capture helpers suid-root, executable by members of the
 "kismet" system group.
 .
 With either option, users who run Kismet must be members of the "kismet"
 group.  If neither is acceptable, Kismet must be run as root.
 .
 This setting is shared by all Kismet capture packages.  If you change it
 with dpkg-reconfigure, apply it to every installed capture helper with:
 .
   dpkg-reconfigure -f noninteractive $(dpkg -l 'kismet-capture-*' | awk '/^ii/ {print $2}')

Template: kismet-common/addgroup-failed
Type: error
Description: Creating the kismet system group failed
 The kismet group does not exist, and creating it failed, so
 Kismet cannot be configured to capture traffic as an unprivileged
 user.
 .
 Please create the kismet system group and try configuring
 kismet-core and any kismet-capture-foo packages again.

Template: kismet-common/setcap-failed
Type: error
Description: Setting capabilities for a Kismet capture tool failed
 The attempt to use Linux capabilities to grant packet-capturing
 privileges to a kismet_cap binary failed.  Instead, it has had the
 set-user-id bit set.
